---
const structures = [
  ["Simple", "One main clause. It may still have long phrases.", "The tall runner in the blue shirt stumbled.", "One main clause"],
  ["Compound", "Two or more main clauses, often joined by and, but or or.", "The runner stumbled, but she recovered.", "Two main clauses"],
  ["Complex", "A main clause plus at least one subordinate clause.", "Although the runner stumbled, she recovered.", "Main + subordinate"]
];
const patterns = [
  ["The dog and the cat slept.", "One main clause with a joined subject.", "Simple"],
  ["The dog barked and scratched the door.", "One subject with two actions.", "Simple"],
  ["The dog barked, and the cat hid.", "Two main clauses.", "Compound"],
  ["The cat hid because the dog barked.", "Main clause plus a reason clause.", "Complex"]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Sentence Structure - Simple, compound, complex</h1>
  <p>Classify by how the clauses are built. Count clauses, not words.</p>
  <section class="summary-block summary-sand">
    <h2>By clause structure</h2>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Structure</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">Rule</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          structures.map(([name, rule, example]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{name}</th>
              <td>{rule}</td>
              <td>{example}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-mint">
    <h2>Test the pattern</h2>
    <p>Find each verb and its subject. Then test whether each part can stand alone.</p>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Sentence</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">Why?</th>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Type</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          patterns.map(([sentence, why, type]) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{sentence}</td>
              <td>{why}</td>
              <td>{type}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
  <div class="trap-card trap-compact">
    <section class="trap">
      <h2>Length, and the word and</h2>
      <p>
        “The tall runner in the blue shirt with muddy shoes crossed the field” is still
        <strong>simple</strong>. The extras are phrases.
      </p>
      <p>
        “Ava packed her bag and closed the door” is one clause: one subject, two actions. “Ava packed her
        bag, and Ben closed the door” is <strong>compound</strong>: two main clauses.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
